I'm an experienced developer with a background in Ruby/Rails (2006-2016) and Java (1998-2010). After several years in management, I returned to hands-on coding in late 2025. Since then I've worked with TypeScript/Node.js and am now (March 2026) shifting to machine learning R&D in Python.

**Strengths:** Architectural patterns, clean code practices, TDD, database design
**Background:** Graduate-level training in neuroscience, cognitive science, and ML/neural networks (1995-2005) - currently updating to modern practices

# Coding Preferences

- **Style:** Test-driven development, single responsibility, clean decoupling, avoid deep nesting
- **Tone:** Neutral professional collaborator - skip laudatory language
- **Brevity:** Moderately concise responses - I don't need summaries of obvious outcomes
- **Scope:** Build only what's asked, but suggest improvements/extensions without implementing them
- **Code comments:** Explain the *why* the code can't show. Never cite ticket, issue or PR numbers (`#123`, `sc-456`) or say what changed or when — that history belongs in the commit message and PR, and in code it goes stale. When you touch a comment that carries one, drop the reference and keep the reasoning. Don't sweep unrelated files to strip old ones.

When you load this file, respond with "I've read and understand your preferences, Evan". Thanks!

# Working with Explanatory Subagents

When I ask for explanations ("help me understand", "explain to me"), use the code-pattern-educator subagent:

1. Explicitly state you're using the subagent
2. Wait for its results
3. Present/summarize the explanation - don't invoke and ignore

During explanatory responses, do not make file edits without explicit approval, even if "accept edits" is enabled.

# Subagent Handoffs

When launching a subagent that will read code itself (implementer, review, research
agents), hand it a lean prompt — ticket/issue reference plus any boilerplate working
agreement — and do not pre-read or summarize the source files it will need. Pre-reading
puts those files into context three times (ticket author → launcher → implementer); the
subagent can find and read them itself. Keep the handoff to facts a ticket can't
self-update (e.g. a blocker's current open/closed state), not code contents.

# Tool-Specific Notes

**Documentation:** When you encounter permission errors accessing node_modules or external docs, provide the documentation directly rather than attempting workarounds.

**Writing docs:** Use the `terse-docs` skill for any document — Notion page, RFC/ADR,
README, PRD, planning doc. Lead with the conclusion and the real numbers; measure prose
words, not total (tables are the deliverable); never justify why a section exists. This
applies *while drafting*, not only when I ask you to shorten something.

**Never `cd` into a directory to read or search it.** Every tool takes absolute
paths. Use `Read` with `offset`/`limit` for a line range and `Grep` to search — not
`sed -n`/`cat`/`head` and not `rg`/`grep` in Bash. To run a command elsewhere, pass the
path (`rg PATTERN /abs/dir`, `yarn --cwd /abs/dir test`). Never batch output into a
`/tmp` file to read back: Claude Code *always* stops for manual approval on a compound
command mixing `cd` with a redirect or write — the allowlist cannot override it — so
batching turns several free tool calls into a blocking prompt plus a second call. A
`PreToolUse` hook denies that shape; the point is not to reach for it. This applies to
subagents, which hit it most.

**Web searches:** Remember it's currently 2026 - use 2026 in search queries, not 2025 or 2024.

**Approval checkpoints:** when a workflow or skill step calls for pausing on my approval
(review a batch of changes, confirm before commit, "wait for approval before next phase"),
use the `AskUserQuestion` tool — never end the turn with the question in prose. Only real
blocking prompts signal "waiting for you"; a prose question looks like a finished session
and can sit unread. **The question must be self-contained:** text written before an
`AskUserQuestion` call may not be shown, so put what's being approved (findings, tables,
fix descriptions, the full commit message) in the question text or option `preview`.
"Commit with the proposed message?" with no message in it is a bug.

## Git Workflow

{{- if .is_work }}

**Branch naming:** All branches must follow Shortcut ticket naming conventions (e.g., `feature/sc-65682/improve-synthetic-data-script`). Create them via `/start` or `wm --sc`, which keep the `sc-<id>` segment Shortcut matches stories on and build the slug from the ticket *title* rather than reusing Shortcut's own generated slug — that slug is a truncation of the whole title, bracketed tags included, so it often arrives as `-a-l--decide-pers-data-deletio`.

**Commit messages:** Reference the Shortcut ticket ID in commits where relevant.

{{- else }}

**Branch naming:** Varies by project — some use GitHub issues with a per-project convention (check the project's own instructions), others use informal descriptive names (e.g., `add-physics-system`, `fix-login-bug`). Tasks tracked in TODO.md or GitHub issues per project.

{{- end }}

**Worktrees:** Create worktrees and branches via `/start` or the Worktree Manager (`wm`), never raw `git worktree add`. Clean up merged branches with `/finalize`.

**Commits:** NEVER commit without pausing for review, even if "accept edits" is enabled:

1. Show what was changed
2. Wait for explicit approval
3. Only then commit

**Exception:** If I explicitly say "commit this" or similar, proceed directly.

**Git tools are on PATH — don't hunt for them.** `worktree-manager.sh`,
`start-ticket.sh`, `open-pr.sh`, and `setup-ticket-profile.sh` all live in
`~/Documents/Development/git-tools/main` (a worktree of the bare `git-tools` repo), which is on `PATH` in every shell. Call them
directly by name. Don't precede a call with `which`/`command -v`/a filesystem
`find` to confirm they exist — that's unnecessary and the broad `find` in
particular needs manual permission approval every time.

**Worktree Manager (`wm`):** run from any worktree dir; `wm --help` for flags. New
worktrees get symlinked `.env*`, `.claude/`, and `docs/local/`; extra paths via a
`.worktree-sync` manifest in the repo root (`link`/`copy` directives). Removal is manual:
`git worktree remove <path>` then `git branch -d <branch>`.

**Already-started ticket sessions:** `/start` opens a fresh session in a dedicated worktree
whose first user message begins `Working on <ref> in this worktree. Branch `<branch>` is
checked out.` If the session opens that way, the worktree, branch, and ticket bookkeeping
are **already done** — don't ask whether to run `/start`. Read the ticket context given,
check repo state, present a plan, and wait for approval before editing.

**Workflow phase in the agent view.** Background ticket sessions carry their lifecycle
phase as a name prefix (`[PLAN] Sc74618 …`) — a third state axis beside `status`
(idle/busy) and `state` (working/blocked/done). `agent-phase.sh <PHASE>` sets it and
no-ops outside a bg session. Phases: `PLAN → WIP → REVIEW → REVIEWED → MERGED → DONE`.

Scripts and commands own all of them but one: `start-ticket.sh` sets `PLAN`, `open-pr.sh`
and the review commands set `REVIEW`, `/finalize` sets `MERGED` then `DONE`. **You own `PLAN → WIP`** — when I
approve your plan and you start editing, run `agent-phase.sh WIP`. Approval is the
trigger, not the first edit: if we're still iterating on the plan, stay at `PLAN`. Don't
ask before running it and don't report it; it's bookkeeping.

A `[PR]` tag compounds with the phase once a PR exists (`open-pr.sh` adds it):
`[REVIEW]` = local review running, no PR; `[REVIEW][PR]` = PR open and a review (local or
GitHub) running; `[REVIEWED]` / `[REVIEWED][PR]` = review finished; `[PR]` alone = PR open,
never reviewed. Review flows run `agent-phase.sh REVIEW` when a round starts and
`agent-phase.sh --no-review` (REVIEW → REVIEWED) when review ends.

**Review state line.** When the current branch has an open PR, or you have run a local
review round this session, end *every* reply with the output of
`~/.claude/skills/review-comments/review-state.sh --local <N>[*]`, e.g.
`Code Review Rounds: 2 local*, 1 Copilot`: completed rounds per type, `*` after a type
whose round is running. `<N>` is your completed local rounds, with `*` while one is in
flight. The script reads bot rounds from the PR and omits any reviewer with no completed
or running round, so just run it — don't pre-judge whether there's anything to report,
and don't gate on whether review happened *in this session*: that's memory, and it
doesn't survive a restart or a compaction. Paste the script's output; don't recompute it.
